From a11390f63a8116752775a2ca32ecc9ae19d475a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:58:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades] VideoPlayer: HEVC progressive by the picture timing
 of its first access units

A HEVC stream whose VUI has frame_field_info_present_flag is not known to be progressive from its
parameter sets (PROGRESSIVE_VIDEO::Check()): FFmpeg's decoder marks a picture as interlaced where the
pic_struct of its picture timing SEI says a field. Such a stream never got a mode at its own rate (25
Hz for 25p), the whole-number multiples, or "Video at its own rate with VRR": an HDR10+ film on a
Samsung Odyssey G60SD ("not known to be progressive: HEVC:
frame_field_info_present_flag is 1"). The flag is common in remuxes of discs, where every picture is a
frame.

Now the demuxer reads the picture timing of the first access units of such a stream, without decoding,
before the streams go to the player (CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::PrefetchForPictureTiming(), as
PrefetchForSoftTelecine() reads the flags of MPEG-2 pictures, and with the packets that the reads ahead
before it took): where 24 access units with their timing all say a whole progressive picture (pic_struct
0, 7 or 8, from a source that is not interlaced) and none says otherwise
(PROGRESSIVE_VIDEO::CPictureTimingProof, HevcPictureTiming()), the stream is progressive
(CDemuxStreamVideo::progressiveByTiming, CDVDStreamInfo::progressive). The first choice of a mode has
it: one change of mode, not a second one once pictures come. A field (pic_struct 1, 2, 9 to 12), a frame
of two fields (3 to 6) or an interlaced source denies it at once, and too few access units read in 1.5 s
leave it as before. A picture marked as interlaced later still makes the stream not progressive for the
rest of the file, as for any stream (CRenderManager::UpdateProgressive()).

Only a file read from its start by a plain input (not a disc, a live stream or a transport stream whose
streams are made as they come), and not where the two tracks of a Dolby Vision stream are joined. Native
Dolby Vision output does not take it (CVideoPlayer::OpenVideoStream()): it keeps the cadence it always
had. kodi.log says what the picture timing said for each such stream.

And only where its verdict can change Kodi's choice of a mode (PROGRESSIVE_VIDEO::PictureTimingNotNeeded()):
with "Adjust display refresh rate" on, and "Video at its own rate with VRR" not off, or whole-number
multiples with a whitelist of the user, or, without one, a frame rate whose own rate, twice it or 2.5
times it is a refresh rate of 30 Hz or less but 24 Hz (25p, 29.97p and 30p, which Kodi's default
whitelist gives a mode of their own only when they are progressive). Elsewhere the reading ahead would
delay the start for nothing: a film at 23.976 with the VRR of videos off, the default, takes no time
for it, and kodi.log says why its timing was not read. Where the settings or the frame rate are not
known, it is read.

Checked: a host test (picture_timing_test.cpp: parameter sets and access units made bit by bit, with
emulation prevention, length fields of 1 to 4 bytes and Annex B; the proof; when it is read, both
ways) with its mutants, built with
ProgressiveVideo.cpp and the FFmpeg headers of the build folder; the changed files and the sources that
include them compile with the commands of the image's build. Not run on a stream in Kodi.
